\[
\Delta S = \sum n__{f \text{ (products)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (products)} - \sum n_{\text{f (reactants)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (reactants)}
\]

where \( n_f \) is the number of moles and \( \Delta S_{\text{f}} \) is the free energy change (Gibbs energy change) for the reaction. However, the question asks for the change in entropy (ΔS), not Gibbs energy change. Thus, we use the correct formula:

\[
\Delta S = \sum n_{\text{f (products)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (products)} - \sum n_{\text{f (reactants)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (reactants)}
\]

The reaction is:
\[
3 \text{CO} + O_2 \rightarrow 3 \text{CO}_2
\]

The given values for the change in enthalpy (ΔH) and entropy (ΔS) at 298 K are:
- \(\Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (CO)} = -208.0 \text{ J/mol K}\)
- \(\Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (O_2)} = 0 \text{ J/mol K}\) (since it is an inert gas)
- \(\Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (CO}_2 \text{)} = -227.0 \text{ J/mol K}\)

The number of moles in the reaction is:
- 3 moles of CO
- 1 mole of O\(_2\)
- 3 moles of CO$_2$

Using the formula:
\[
\Delta S = \sum n_{\text{f (products)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (products)} - \sum n_{\text{f (reactants)}} \Delta S_{\text{f}} \text{ (reactants)}
\]
\[
\Delta S = (3 \times (- 227.0 \text{ J/mol K}) + 1 \times (0 \text{ J/mol K})) - (3 \times (- 208.0 \text{ J/mol K} + 1 \times (0 \text{ J/mol K}))^)
\]
\[
\Delta S = ((- 681.0 + 0) - (- 628.0 + 0)) = - 681.0 - - 628.0 = - 681.0 + 628.0 = - -53.0 \text{ J/K}
\]
\[
\Delta S = -53.0 \text{ J/K}
\]

Thus, the change in entropy for the reaction is \(\boxed{-53.0 \text{ J/K}}\).
