<USER_REQUEST>
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Distance} \times \text{cos}(\theta)
\]  
where:  
- Force is the force applied (60.0 N)  
- Distance is the distance over which the force is applied (10.0 m)  
- \theta is the angle between the force and the distance (60.0°)  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times \cos(60^\circ)
\]  
Since \(\cos(60^\circ) = 0.5\):  
\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \times 10.0 \times 0.5 = 600 \times 0.5 = 360 \text{ J}
\]  
Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \text{ J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

