</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The work done by a gas is given by the change in pressure multiplied by the change in volume: \( W = P \Delta V \). Since the piston is open to the atmosphere, the pressure inside the cylinder is equal to the atmospheric pressure \( P__{a} \). The isothermal expansion means the temperature \( T \) remains constant.

The initial volume is \( V__1 \). The final volume is \( n \times V_1 \), so \( V_2 = n V_1 \). The change in volume is \( \Delta V = V_2 - V_1 = n V_1 - V_1 = (n - 1) V_1 \).

The pressure remains constant, equal to the atmospheric pressure \( P_j \). Thus, the work done is:
\[ W = P_j \Delta V = P_j ((n - 1) y_1) = (n - 1) P_j y_1 \).

The work done is \( (n - 1) P_j y_1 \), where \( P_j \) is the atmospheric pressure and \( V_1 \) is the initial volume.

The work done is:
\[ W = P_j (n V_1 - y_1) = P_j (n - 1) y_1 \).

The work done is \( (n - 1) P y_1 \), where \( P \) is the atmospheric pressure and \( V_1 \) is the initial volume.

Final Answer:
\[(n - 1) P V_1\]
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

