\]

However, the more common and useful form is:

\[
\lambda^2 \gamma^2 = \frac{2.5 \times 10^{-10}{10^10^ \lambda \ln \frac{T}{\text{T}_\text{ref}}}}{\gamma}\)
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the radiation.
- \(\gamma\) is the intensity (or intensity per unit area) of the radiation at that wavelength.
- \(T\) is the absolute temperature of the black body (in Kelvin).
- \(\lambda__0 = 10^{-10} \text{ m}}\).
- \(\text{T}_\text{ref} = 2.74 \times 10^27 \text{ K}\).

