The standard free energy change for this reaction is calculated using the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 1 atm and 25°C (\(\Delta G^^0 = -38.5 \text{ kJ/mol}\)). The standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C is calculated using the Gibbs equation:
\[
\Delta G = \Delta G^^0 - 0.0299 \times 298 \times (T - 298)
\]
where:
- $\Delta G$ is the standard free energy change at 37°C (29.8 K)
- $\Delta G^0)$ is the standard free energy change at 29.8 K (-38.5 kJ/mol)
- 0.0299 is the gas constant (J/mol·K)
- 298 is the reference temperature (K)
