The work done by the friction force is \( \Delta W = -F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta \Delta \text{FR} \), where \( \Delta \Delta \text{FR} \) is the change in friction force. Since the work is dissipated as heat, the change in energy is \( \Delta E = \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{2} \text{FR} \). The work done by the friction is equal to the change in energy, so \( \Delta E = \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{2} \text{FR} \).

The change in entropy is given by the entropy of the system (the hands) and the environment. The work done by the friction force is converted into energy, which is distributed to the system and the environment as heat. The change in entropy is given by:
\[ \Delta S = \frac{Q}{T} \]
where \( Q \) is the heat transferred and \( T \) is the absolute temperature. In this case, \( Q = \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{2} \text{FR} \). Thus,
