\[
\lambda_{\text{diff}} = \lambda__0 / (n_f - n_{\text{eff}})
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_{\text{diff}}\) is the decay length.
- \(\lambda_0\) is the wavelength of the wave.
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the front medium (in this case, the Ga\(_2\)Al\(^3.2}\)As cladding).
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the composite structure (Ga\(^3.2}\)Al\(^3.2}\)As cladding and Ga\(3.2}\)Al\(^3.2}\)As core).

The given information is:
- \(\lambda_0 = 1 \mu\text{m} = 10^6 \text{ nm}\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\)  
Thus, \(n_f - n_{\text{eff}} = -0.3\)

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
\lambda_{\text{diff}} = 1000 \text{ nm} / 0.3 = 1366 \text{ nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{1366 \text{ nm}}\).
