","The hydrolysis of ATP is the process where ATP is broken down into ADP and phosphate (Pi). The reaction is:
\[
\text{ATP} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{ADP} + \text{P}_i \quad \text{(ATP hydrolysis)}
\]
The standard free energy change for this reaction is calculated using the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 1 atm and 25°C (\(\Delta G^^0 = -38.5 \text{ kJ/mol}\)). The standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C is calculated using the Gibbs equation:
\[
\Delta G = \Delta G^^0 - 0.0299 \times 298 \times (T - 298)
\]
where:
- $\Delta G$ is the standard free energy change at 37°C (29.8 K)
- $\Delta G^0)$ is the standard free energy change at 29.8 K (-38.5 kJ/mol)
- 0.0299 is the gas constant (J/mol·K)
- 298 is the reference temperature (K)
- \( T \) is the temperature (K)

\[
\Delta G = -38.5 \text{ kJ/mol} - 0.0299 \times 298 \times (37.0 - 29.8)
\]
\[
\Delta G = -38.5 \text{ kJ/mol} - 0.0299 \times 298 \times 7.2
\]
\[\Delta G = -38.5 - 1.01 \text{ kJ/mol} = -39.51 \text{ kJ/mol} \approx -39.5 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

Thus, the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C is \(\boxed{-39.5 \text{ kJ/mol}}\).

**Key Point**:
The hydrolysis of ATP is exothermic (negative \(\Delta G\)) and irreversible under cellular conditions.

